eally for  the first time in which China and Russia  were to sign onto these and unanimously  condemn North Korea and sanction North  Korea for their actions so in the short  term I think you that was a game changer  and since then every time the North  Koreans have done something egregious  for the most part the Chinese and the  Russians  and certainly gone long I said certainly  when it comes to nuclear tests but in  the longer term in retrospect that was  about the only real change it didn't  create this sort of game changing  mentality in the way the Chinese dealt  with North Korea you know there I think  there was a lot of debate in North in  China as to whether they should simply  drop this ally dropped this this legacy  of the Cold War and really helped to end  this regime that clearly does not happen  if anything the Chinese have drawn  closer to North Korea over the past few  years both economically and in terms of  politically supporting the new  leadership so in the end it didn't  create the sort of major change that  people thought it would and they get  part of it I think is the fact that the  status quo bias if you will in terms of  dealing with the North Korean and the  crisis is to try to just bring it back  down to try to lower the temperature it  is not to try to solve the problem and  and that is a political choice I think  that administration's in Washington and  Seoul in Tokyo and Beijing into Moscow  these are choices that they make because  in the end at least for now stability  equates to peace and prosperity in Asia  status quo equates to peace and  prosperity in East Asia you know the  most economically vibrant region of the  world and do people want that or do they  want to go down this very violent path  with North Korea potentially very  violent path with North Korea where you  try to solve the problem it's very clear  what every government wants to do they  want to maintain the status quo  well and that really brings us to the  question of Korean unification where it  seems that there's a big gap between the  u.s. vision and a Chinese vision and  also it involves what South Koreans want  in the future my impression was that you  were pretty bullish about the prospects  for achieving unification you also dealt  and to some degree with some of the  challenge  that would emerge you know at this point  you know how likely do you think it is  that Korea will actually be able to  achieve unification and and and how do  you see that process playing out in  terms of you know basically in a context  where China's influence continues to  rise yeah well I think you're I mean  your question is right I mean I don't  think the United States and South Korea  on the one hand and China on the other  hand have the same view of unification  the United States has said explicitly in  the joint statements of the past two  presidents with their South Korean  counterparts that the u.s. aspiration is  for a Korea free and at peace a single  Korea free and at peace that is the  natural order of things in the  international relations of this part of  the world a unified Korean Peninsula and  the Chinese don't the Chinese don't want  to see unification  they just don't and I think that's  become clearer and clearer in the past  couple of a couple of years I think  there really is a conflict of interest  between the two sides when it comes to  that in terms of the future and  unification it's impossible to say how  it would happen just like no one could  predict how are the conditions under  which German unification would happen  but I think what we can't focus on is  the question of whether countries in the  region are ready and willing to take on  the task of unification and I think ten  years ago that was not the case ten  years ago I think the general consensus  was unification is too difficult much  harder than the two Germany's too  dangerous and should be something that  should be pushed off for as far as you  can into the future to three generations  if you will basically not not my problem  no one wanted it to be their problem and  as I talked about in the book I think  the attitude on that is slowly changing  now in part because the the weapons  situation with North Korea is getting  worse and worse the human rights  situation is getting more  and worse and I think while no one wants  to try to push North Korea over the edge  there is a growing feeling that it is  coming and that we must be prepared and  I think you're seeing that sort of  change of attitude in South Korea I  think you're even seeing in places like  Japan where where the North Korean  threat is the biggest existential threat  to Japan today and while on the one hand  Japanese are always concerned about  reunified resurgent nationalism in a in  in in one Korea they see the current  situation as being quite dangerous  potentially very unstable and I think  their attitudes are changing on this too  so I think that in the book so I you  know we look and talk about sort of  those are the things we need to watch  because no one can predict unification  no one can say exactly when it's going  to happen but you know the question is  are you prepared for it and and that's  the operative question I think for the  societies and the governments in the  region and whereas before no one wanted  to think I talked about it at all I  think there's a much more openness and  willingness to talk about this now  I also want to ask you just to close  after Kim jong-il died you wrote a piece  in The New York Times you basically said  North Korea as we know it is over so far  we see a fair amount of continuity I  guess the question is you know how  durable is the impossible state yeah  well you know it all depends on how you  define North Korea I think North Korea  as we know know it with the death of Kim  Jong Il is over in the sense that as I  talked about in the book I think we're  entering a new phase where you have a  young untested leadership with immense  challenges in terms of maintaining his  own position in the system that is also  dealing with a crumbling economy and in  an in an acute food problem  and at the same time the society is  increasingly influenced by a market  mentality like is it like it was not the  last time you in 1994 when the first  leader died and the second leader came  came into place so in that sense the  North Korean today is not like the North  Korea in the past and sure it has not it  has not collapsed or it has not changed  since the death of Kim jong-il but you  know we're only talking a few months I  mean this new regime in North Korea has  only been in power for a few months and  in the broader scope of history there  have been many transitional regimes that  have lasted months years before  something major has happened in terms of  change so I think the the verdict is  still out on this I don't think that we  can simply assume that everything will  go smoothly and that they will be able  to muddle through forever because prior  to the death of Kim Jong Il in December  of 2011 if you had asked any expert I  would say including you Scott what would  be the most important variable that  could create major change in this  country  I think everyone would have agreed the  sudden death of the North Korean leader  so I think we have to respect what we  thought before and I think we all have  to watch very carefully what is going to  happen because I think the future of  this regime is not at all certain and  and the ramifications if something  happened will be huge for the United  States and for others in the region in a  way that will matter for the average  American and and and that's why I wanted  to write this book  well thanks Viktor I think that we had a  very good conversation and appreciate  the chance to further explore some of  the issues that are in the book thanks  very much Scott
 

